==English==
{{was wotd|2009|October|9}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʰed-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|begeten}} [influenced by Old Norse ''[[geta]]'' ("to get, to guess")], from {{der|en|ang|beġietan||to get}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*bigetaną||to find, seize}}, equivalent to {{af|en|be-|get}}. Cognate with {{cog|osx|bigetan||to find, seize}}, {{cog|goh|bigezan||to gain, achieve, win, procure}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/biˈɡɛt/|/bɪˈɡɛt/|/bəˈɡɛt/|a=UK,US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-beget.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛt|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|~s|~ting|begot,begat<l:archaic>|begotten,begot<l:rare>}} {{tlb|en|transitive}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|literary}} To [[produce]] or bring forth (a [[child]]); to be a [[parent]] of; to [[father]] or [[sire]].
#: {{ux|en|The King intends to '''beget''' a child within the next five years.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Genesis|5|3|passage=¶ And Adam liued an hundred and thirtie yeeres, and '''begate''' a ſonne in his owne likeneſſe, after his image; and called his name Seth.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=William H. Frist; Shirley Wilson|title=Good People Beget Good People: A Genealogy of the Frist Family|publisher=Rowman & Littlefield|isbn=9780742533363|page=110
|passage=I believe good people '''beget''' good people. If you marry the right person, then you  will have good children. But everywhere else in life, too, good people beget good  people. In your work, when you hire good people, they, in turn, will hire good&nbsp;...}}
# To [[cause]]; to [[produce]]; to [[bring forth]].
#: {{syn|en|breed|}}
#: {{ux|en|Wealth '''begets''' wealth; poverty '''begets''' poverty.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Pendennis||passage=How one’s thoughts will travel! and how quickly our wishes '''beget''' them!|2}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|author=Henry Alford|title=Future TED Talks|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/05/06/opinion/sunday/future-ted-talks.html|date=2012-05-05|archivedate=2012-05-09|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120509054938/https://www.nytimes.com/2012/05/06/opinion/sunday/future-ted-talks.html|passage=In the same way that Old Europe’s coffeehouses begat insurance companies, he says, today’s political careers '''beget''' an unhealthy relationship with throat lozenges.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To get or obtain.
#* {{RQ:Jonson Bartholomew Fair|Induction|text=If there bee neuer a ''Seruant-monſter'' i' the ''Fayre'', who can helpe it, he ſayes ; nor a neſt of ''Antiques ?'' &ensp; Hee is loth to make Nature afraid in his ''Playes'', like thoſe that '''beget''' ''Tales'', ''Tempeſts'', and ſuch like ''Drolleries'', {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|UK|_|dialectal}} To [[happen]] to; [[befall]].
{{en-conj|past=begot|past_old=begat|pres_ptc=begetting|past_ptc=begotten|past_ptc2=begot|past_ptc2_qual=[[Appendix:Glossary#rare|rare]]}}

